::STAT_U02_L04_Q01::
Question ID: STAT_U02_L04_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DVD.4
Question:
A distribution has a tail stretching toward larger values. What is the shape?
{
~A. Skewed left#Left skew has a tail toward smaller values.
~B. Symmetric#Symmetric distributions are balanced.
~C. No variability#A skewed distribution can still have variability.
=D. Skewed right#The direction of skew follows the tail.
}

::STAT_U02_L04_Q02::
Question ID: STAT_U02_L04_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DVD.4
Question:
A distribution is balanced around its center. What is it called?
{
=A. Symmetric#Symmetry means the shape is balanced around the center.
~B. Skewed right#Right skew has a long right tail.
~C. Skewed left#Left skew has a long left tail.
~D. Outlier#An outlier is an unusually far value.
}

::STAT_U02_L04_Q03::
Question ID: STAT_U02_L04_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DVD.4
Question:
What is a cluster in a data distribution?
{
=A. A group of data values close together#A cluster is where many values are concentrated.
~B. A value far from the rest#That is an outlier.
~C. An interval with no values#That is a gap.
~D. The largest value only#The largest value alone is not a cluster.
}

::STAT_U02_L04_Q04::
Question ID: STAT_U02_L04_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DVD.4
Question:
What is a gap in a data distribution?
{
~A. A group of close values#That is a cluster.
=B. An interval with no data values#A gap is a space in the distribution where no values occur.
~C. The most common value#That is the mode.
~D. A value far from all others#That is a possible outlier.
}

::STAT_U02_L04_Q05::
Question ID: STAT_U02_L04_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DVD.4
Question:
In a skewed-right distribution, why can the mean be larger than the median?
{
~A. The median uses every value equally#The median is based on position, not every value equally.
~B. Skewed right means no high values#Skewed right means the tail extends to high values.
=C. The right tail can pull the mean upward#Extreme high values affect the mean more than the median.
~D. The mode must equal the range#Mode and range are different summaries.
}

